title: OTHER_SKGREPO_DEPENDENCIES.toml
pid: 3f80ee69-ca8f-4d93-96bb-d719f73cc60a
body: |2-
  At every server start the server writes an =OTHER_SKGREPO_DEPENDENCIES.toml=
  manifest into each owned skgrepo directory. Its =dependencies= is a
  list of pairs, one per rung of the privacy ladder through that
  skgrepo — itself and every more public owned skgrepo, in privacy
  order, the skgrepo itself last. Foreign skgrepos never appear in it:
  an owned skgrepo does not depend on one (a relationship to a foreign
  node is recorded in the owned file and names an ID, not an order).
  Each pair has a =path= (data-root-relative directory) and, unless the
  skgrepo is not a gitrepo or has no remote, a =git-remote= (the fetch
  URL =git remote -v= prints, =origin= preferred), so a receiver knows
  where to fetch each dependency; when the chosen remote is not =origin=
  its name is also recorded as =git-remote-name=. skg makes no use of
  the =git-remote= values itself. For example:

  #+begin_src toml
  dependencies = [
    { path = "me/public", git-remote = "git@github.com:me/public.git" },
    { path = "me/private", git-remote = "git@github.com:me/private.git" },
  ]
  #+end_src

  It is generated output — hand edits are clobbered — and lets someone
  who receives one of your skgrepos see what it depends on and where to get
  it.

  A receiver reads the manifest of each of its foreign skgrepos when
  its config loads. An entry names the receiver's foreign skgrepo at
  the same data-root-relative path, else the one foreign skgrepo whose
  folder has the same name (the receiver may keep a publisher's
  skgrepos in another author folder); an entry matching neither way, or
  several foreign skgrepos by folder name, is skipped. With each
  config in its owner's folder, a manifest's paths are relative to the
  publisher's folder (=public=, not =claude/public=), so a receiver
  matches them by folder name; foreign skgrepos of different owners
  that share a folder name are matched by neither. The manifests do
  two things:
  - *Ownership.* Foreign skgrepos that manifests chain together (a
    manifest's own skgrepo and every one it lists, transitively) are
    one owner's ('SkgrepoOwner', 'SkgConfig::skgrepo_owner'); foreign
    skgrepos no manifest chains are different owners'. Only one owner's
    sections of a node fold together; the rest are illegal edits.
  - *Fold order.* A foreign node's sections fold in its owner's order:
    a pair that some manifest lists, and no manifest lists the other way
    round, in the manifest's order; a pair two manifests disagree about,
    as the manifest of the more private one says (the one whose own
    manifest lists the other; failing that, the one listed first in the
    config); and any other pair in declaration order, so far as the ordered
    pairs allow.

  The server warns at startup about a disagreeing pair when some node
  of that owner has a section in each, naming the node (its ID and
  title, and how many more nodes span the pair), both skgrepos, the
  disagreeing manifests, and which one this server folds first; it
  appears at init, in the log and in the startup warnings each client
  shows on connect (see "Server warnings", above). (The receiver also
  reads the bare-string =dependencies= shape.)
